      "id": 1,
      "text": "Oakdale was originally called \"Honeycutt\" after early settler Allen Honeycutt.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oakdale%2C_Tennessee",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In the 1880s, the Cincinnati Southern Railway ran through Oakdale, meeting the East Tennessee, Virginia and Georgia Railroad at Emory Gap near Harriman.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oakdale%2C_Tennessee"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "By the early 1900s, Oakdale boasted a bank, five general stores, a drugstore, a hardware store, three schools, two churches, six secret societies, and a newspaper.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oakdale%2C_Tennessee"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town’s large hotel, the Babahatchie Inn, opened in 1880, burned in 1892, was rebuilt, and became a YMCA with 1,500 beds in 1905.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oakdale%2C_Tennessee"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Oakdale sits on the Emory River between river miles 17 and 19, atop the Cumberland Plateau, and is split into two areas on either side of the river.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
